>> title: Birista
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: indian, onion, fried, garnish, component
>> counters: Curry House, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: birishta, fried onions, crisp fried onions, barista onions, talay huay pyaaz
>> pairs-with: biryani
>> servings: 1 1/2 cups
>> time: 50 min
>> step.1: salt and rest 20 min, then squeeze dry
>> step.2: fry 20 min, until the pale ones have gone amber
>> step.3: drain 5 min on paper

Rest @yellow onions{1%lb}(450 g; halved pole to pole and sliced paper-thin) under @kosher salt{2%tsp} ~rest{20%min}, then take them up by the handful and squeeze out the water. Wet onions steam in the oil and come out leathery, and this is the step everyone skips.

Fry @&(~1)squeezed onions{} in @neutral oil{1%cup}(240 mL) and @ghee{2%Tbs}(28 g) in a wide #pan{} over medium heat ~fry{20%min}, stirring often, until the palest strands have gone amber — then lift them out at once, because they carry on darkening off the heat and burnt onion is bitter all the way through.

Drain @&(~1)fried onions{} in one layer on paper ~drain{5%min}. They crisp as they cool, not in the pan.

This is biryani's middle layer and mujaddara's top one, and the fried-onion oil left in the pan is worth keeping — it is the best thing to start a dal in. Airtight, they hold a fortnight.
